An independent check read every line of the first draft against the episode files and found 13 problems, three of them serious: a casebook thread and a Garrick line that pointed too near the secret, and a click that answered its own night's question. Those fixes stand, further down. The 21 Oct pass then changed four questions' bookkeeping, one click, one lead, two nudges, and the cup, so a later line is never called an answer when it is only a turn.
How to read each night
Tonight's question is what the fire pins and calls at dawn. It is still open when the fire opens, and the story answers it later. Read back says where, and MYTHIOS sets the party's call beside that line. A line marked Turn is heard, and nobody is graded. Only a line marked Answer is the read-back.
A friend who is alone, including every friend on Day 1, still pins. There is no one to peek at. The morning speaks in the first person: Last night you called it: and then their own line.
Tonight's puzzle is a smaller question the evidence can settle tonight. When the two pieces that click are both on the table, laid by two different friends, MYTHIOS says what they show together. If nobody completes the pair, the next morning MYTHIOS shows the same line, as what the table would have shown. So no puzzle is ever left hanging. A click is built from two ordinary finds, never from a [PRESS ON] roll.
The pieces are the three last-light leads, plus the pass line and the [PRESS ON] find where they bear on the night. Each has a job (Reveal, Tilt, Turn, Link) and a payoff. Notice lines stay as they are; they are pieces too, but a click never depends on one.
Garrick speaks only where shown. A nudge that names a piece speaks only once that piece is on the table; otherwise its plain version is used. He never investigates and never concludes. On a cup night he pours, and he says the line he already says. He does not explain the cup.
Text marked NEW is new. Everything else is already in the game, word for word.
